course: dictionary
lesson: 7661
title: Saying words out loud
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Pronunciation is the way a word is said out loud. It is also how a person says words, including their accent, which is the special way their voice sounds. «Pinyin writes Chinese sounds with Latin letters and tone marks; it helps learners with pronunciation.» Pinyin shows learners how to say Chinese words, not just how they look.
explain: English spelling does not always show how a word is said. Through, though and tough end in the same letters, but they sound different. So you cannot always work out how to say a word just by looking at it.
explain: People in different places can pronounce the same word in different ways. People in the UK, USA and Australia may say tomato differently, and each way can be right for the people who use it.
fact: Pronunciation means the way a word is said out loud.
fact: Pronunciation also means how a person says words, including their accent.
fact: English spelling does not always match pronunciation: through, though and tough look alike but sound different.
fact: Received Pronunciation (RP) is the traditional "posh" accent once used on the BBC.
fact: «Most British people do not speak the "posh" BBC accent (Received Pronunciation)»; it is often estimated that only about 2 or 3 in every 100 people speak it naturally, and the rest have regional accents.
exercise: «Pinyin writes Chinese sounds with Latin letters and tone marks; it helps learners with pronunciation.» What does Pinyin help learners with? | Writing very long stories | Reading faster than anyone else | Drawing pictures of Chinese characters | *Saying Chinese sounds correctly
exercise: Ella asks why «through», «though» and «tough» look alike but sound different. What is the best answer? | *English spelling does not always match how words are said | Teachers choose the sounds at random | They are written in Chinese letters | They all mean the same thing
exercise: People in the UK, USA and Australia may say «tomato» differently. What does this show? | *Different people can have different pronunciations of the same word | Tomatoes only grow in Australia | Tomato is not a real word | Everyone says tomato in exactly the same way
test: What does pronunciation mean? | A word that rhymes with another word | Writing a word down neatly | A list of tricky spellings | *The way a word is said out loud
test: Pronunciation can also tell us about a person. What can it show? | *Their accent | Their height | Their favourite colour | Their shoe size
exam: «Most British people do not speak the "posh" BBC accent (Received Pronunciation)»; which is true? | Most British people speak Received Pronunciation | *Most British people have regional accents | Nobody in Britain has an accent | Every British person speaks in exactly the same way
